From 39bed432cdf669650ab9689a53c2c7cc807e1184 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dispatch Sandbox <jax1313@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 19:41:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 07/12] Give the operator a way to take a backup, and to prove
 one

926 lines of backup engine, a manifest, SHA-256 verification, a safe restore
that repoints stored file paths, and a status reporter -- and nothing in the
product that took one. No portal route, no template, no launcher command, no
scheduled task. The only in-product caller of create_backup() was
dispatch/proof.py, whose own commands did not run. The whole estate -- SQLite,
twelve JSON stores and every uploaded evidence file -- sat on one laptop behind
Mike remembering a script path that appeared in no menu.

Added, all calling the same engine so there is one implementation and one answer:

  [B] Back Up Now / [R] Prove a Restore   on the launcher menu (lettered,
                                          per the convention the eight
                                          numbered controls established)
  python -m dispatch_launcher backup | prove-restore | schedule-backup
  python scripts/dispatch_backup.py prove <archive> <scratch>
  /maintenance                             in the portal, with both buttons

schedule-backup prints the schtasks and cron lines rather than running them.
Registering a task writes to the machine outside Dispatch's own folders, and the
program does not do that to somebody's computer unasked -- but making the
operator invent the command is how a backup never gets scheduled at all.

The honesty rule in dispatch_launcher/backups.py is kept and is now enforced
rather than merely unreachable. A restore this program performs records
performed_by = Code-automated, and the status stays UNVERIFIED with the detail
upgraded to say what was actually done: the archive restored into an isolated
destination and every hash matched. VERIFIED still requires a person who opened
the restored Dispatch and typed their name. A record with no performed_by at all
predates the program being able to perform one, so it keeps its VERIFIED
meaning -- that field could only have been written by hand.
